## One-step synthesis of dual-ligand 2D conductive metal-organic framework for high-performance lithium storage
ABSTRACT Two-dimensional conductive metal-organic frameworks (2D c-MOFs) are emerging as promising electrode materials for lithium-ion batteries (LIBs). However, 2D c-MOFs with binary redox-active organic ligands are rare and suffer from complicated synthesis procedures. Herein, a facile one-step method is developed for the synthesis of a dual-ligand-based 2D c-MOF \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) (HHTP = 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexahydroxytriphenylene, \(\mathrm{THQ}=\) tetrahydroxy-1,4-benzoquinone) by using the ethylenediamine regulator. The resulting \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) possesses good conductivity, abundant active sites, and excellent chemical stability. As an anode material for LIBs, \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) exhibits high specific capacity, good rate performance, and long-term cycling stability. Comprehensive experimental studies and theoretical calculations reveal that \(\mathrm{CuO}_{4}\) units and aromatic benzene rings in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) are involved in lithium storage, thereby optimizing the lithium storage performance. This work provides a new way for the construction of dual-ligand 2D c-MOFs and high-performance LIB electrode materials.
Keywords: conductive metal-organic frameworks, redox-active sites, dual-ligand, lithium-ion batteries
## INTRODUCTION
Lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) have attracted increasing attention due to their extensive use in advanced electronic equipment, electric vehicles, grid-scale energy-storage systems, etc. [1–4]. However, commercial LIBs made using graphite anodes and transition-metal compounds-based cathodes are approaching their performance limits [5]. Particularly, graphite anodes possess a low theoretical capacity of \(372\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), which cannot meet the ever-growing demand for high-capacity LIBs [6,7]. Therefore, the exploitation of advanced electrode materials is highly desirable for the development of high-performance LIBs.
Two-dimensional conductive metal-organic frameworks (2D c-MOFs), as a class of emerging porous crystalline 2D materials comprised of \(\pi\)-conjugated organic linkers and metal nodes, have been shown to exhibit great potential as electrode materials for rechargeable batteries in recent years [8,9]. 2D c-MOFs integrate the advantages of traditional MOFs in terms of structural tunability and channel variability. The designable and predictable structures give rise to substantial opportunities for integrating distinct multi-functional redox components into the target network, thereby improving the lithium storage performance [10,11]. Furthermore, 2D c-MOFs exhibit an unprecedented degree of high electrical conductivity and porosity, which enables them to provide efficient electron/ion transport pathways and fast charging rates [12]. Besides, their rigid and robust frameworks render excellent stability in organic electrolytes, guaranteeing the electrochemical durability of electrodes [13]. These advantages enable the rapid development of 2D c-MOFs in the field of lithium storage. In 2018, Nishihara and coworkers [14] reported the first 2D c-MOF-based electrode for LIBs, NiDI (DI = hexaiminobenzene), in which \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{L}^{\mathrm{isq}})_{2}\) (L\(^{\mathrm{isq}}\) = d-diiminobenzosemiquinonate) centers can serve as redox active sites demonstrated by series spectroscopic techniques. Since then, a series of 2D c-MOF-based electrode materials have been developed and widely used in rechargeable batteries [15–17]. Nevertheless, state-of-the-art 2D c-MOF-based electrodes are still limited to systems with a single redox-active organic ligand, which restricts their maximum potential in the field of electrochemical energy storage. In contrast, dual-ligand-based 2D c-MOFs possess abundant chemical compositions and tunable topological structures [18,19], which not only overcome the limitation of the few kinds of 2D c-MOFs' ligands but also provide more opportunities for improving the electrochemical performance of 2D c-MOF-based electrode materials. However, due to the competitive coordination effect between different ligands, the construction of dual-ligand-based 2D c-MOFs in one step is very challenging.
