Herein, we developed a facile one-step method to fabricate a dual-ligand 2D c-MOF \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) (HHTP = 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexahydroxytriphenylene, \(\mathrm{THQ}=\) tetrahydroxy-1,4-benzoquinone), in which ethylenediamine was used as a regulator to balance the competitive coordination process between the HHTP and THQ ligands. The resulting \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) delivers good conductivity, abundant active sites, and excellent chemical stability. As an anode material for LIBs, \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})\)-(THQ) shows high specific capacity, good rate performance, and long-term cycling stability. In addition, combining spectral characterizations and theoretical calculations reveals that both \(\mathrm{CuO}_{4}\) and aromatic benzene active sites in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) are involved in lithium storage. This work opens up a new way for the rational design of 2D c-MOFs with dual ligands and
provides a new possibility for the development of high-performance electrode materials.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
## Materials
All reagents and solvents were purchased from commercial sources and directly used without further purification. HHTP was synthesized according to the literature [20].
## Synthesis of \(\mathbf{Cu}_{3}(\mathbf{HHTP})(\mathbf{THQ})\)
HHTP \(\mathrm{[13~mg,}\) , \(0.04\;\mathrm{mmol})\) , THQ \(\left(6.9\;\mathrm{mg},\quad0.04\;\mathrm{mmol}\right)\) , \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \(\left(29\;\mathrm{mg},\ \ 0.12\;\mathrm{mmol}\right)\) , and \(2.0\;\mathrm{mL}\) delonized water were added to a \(10{\-}\mathrm{mL}\) scintillation vial followed by adding \(20~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) ethylenediamine. The mixture was sonicated for \(20\;\mathrm{min}\) at room temperature, and then the vials were sealed and placed in an oven at \(80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(48\,\mathrm{h}\) . After being cooled down to room temperature, the dark solid was filtered and washed with \(N,N.\) -dimethylformamide, deionized water, and ethanol successively. Then the resulting precipitates were dried at \(100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) overnight to obtain the final product of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) (yield: \(24~\mathrm{mg}\) , \(90\%\) ).
## Characterizations
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns were collected on a Rigaku Smart Lab using \(\mathrm{Cu}\,\mathrm{Ko}\) radiation \((\lambda=1.5406\mathrm{~\AA~})\) at \(40\,\mathrm{kV}\) and \(40\;\mathrm{mA}\) . Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra were recorded on a Bruker TENSOR FT-IR spectrometer. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were collected on a Hitachi HT7800 microscope at \(90\;\mathrm{kV}\) . Scanning TEM (STEM) images and the corresponding elemental mapping were captured with a JEOL JEM-2800 TEM at \(200\;\mathrm{kV}\) . Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images and the corresponding energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) spectrum were recorded on a JEOL JSM-7800F with a \(15.0{\-}\mathrm{kV}\) accelerating voltage. Nitrogen \(\left(\mathbf{N}_{2}\right)\) sorption measurement was carried out on a Micromeritics ASAP 2460 instrument at \(77\,\mathrm{K}.\) X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra were collected on a Thermo Scientific ESCALAB 250Xi spectrometer using an Al Kα source. Thermogravimetric analyses (TGA) were conducted on a Rigaku TG8121 analyzer under an argon (Ar) atmosphere with a heating rate of \(10^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) . Electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectra were taken on a Bruker E580-10/12 spectrometer. Electrical conductivity measurement was performed on a Keithley 4200A-SCS parameter analyzer with a two-point probe method. Ultraviolet-visible (UV-vis) spectra were collected on the Shimadzu UV-2600 spectrometer.
## Electrochemical measurements
To prepare the working electrodes, the electroactive materials including \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) , HHTP, and THQ were blended with Ketjen Black (conductive additive) and carboxymethyl cellulose (CMC) binder in a mass ratio of 7:2:1, in which water was used as the solvent. The slurry was thoroughly ground in a mortar for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) and then homogeneously coated onto a \({\mathrm{Cu}}\) foil. After being vacuum-dried at \(80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) , the Cu foil coated with the active material (thickness \({\sim}0.05\ \mathrm{mm}\) ) was cut into circular disks of \(1.2\;\mathrm{cm}\) in diameter, and the active material areal loading was around \(1.0{-}1.2\ \mathrm{mg\cm}^{-2}\) . The electrochemical performance of the electrodes was evaluated in coil cells (CR2032) with a lithium plate \(\left(1.45\;\mathrm{cm}\right)\) as the counter electrode.
Polypropylene film (Celgard 2400) served as a separator to separate the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})/\mathrm{HHTP}/\mathrm{THQ}\) cathode and Li anode, and \(1\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\;\mathrm{LiPF}_{6}\) in ethylene carbonate (EC)/diethyl carbonate (DEC)/dimethyl carbonate (DMC) mixture \((1{:}1{:}1\ \nu/\nu/\) \(\nu)\) \((\sim\!70\,\upmu\mathrm{L})\) was used as electrolyte. The CR2032-type coin cells were assembled in an Ar-filled glove box with water and the oxygen concentration controlled below \(0.1\;\mathrm{ppm}\) . The battery performance tests were conducted in a potential range of 0.01 to \(1.0\;\mathrm{V}\) (versus \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/L i)}\) at various current densities on a LAND battery testing instrument (CT-2001A). The cyclic voltammetry (CV) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were measured on a VersaSTAT 4 electrochemical workstation (Princeton Applied Research).
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
