The elemental compositions of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) were conducted by EDS and the corresponding elemental mapping analysis, and the results reveal that the uniform distribution of C, O, and Cu elements throughout the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) sample (Fig. 1f; Figs S8 and S9). XPS survey spectrum further confirms the presence of C, O, and Cu elements in the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) sample (Fig. S10). In addition, the \(\mathrm{Cu}~2\mathrm{p}\) HR-XPS spectrum shows obvious characteristic signals of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) (934.6 and \(954.4\;\mathrm{eV}\)) and \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\) (932.4 and \(952.1\;\mathrm{eV}\)) (Fig. 2a), which reveals the existence of two different valence states of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) element in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) [24]. The EPR spectrum of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) exhibits a strong symmetrical signal at \(g=2.09\) (Fig. S11), which is the characteristic peak of the paramagnetic \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) radicals, suggesting the existence of \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) [25]. TGA curves reveal the high decomposition temperature of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})\)- (THQ) above \(200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. S12). The robustness of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})\)- (THQ) was further evaluated by soaking the samples in water
and the corresponding electrolyte for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) , and it was found that the treated sample retained nearly the identical PXRD pattern compared with the pristine sample (Fig. 2b), demonstrating the excellent chemical stability of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) .
Given the \(\mathrm{d}{-}\pi\) conjugated structure of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) , its electronic properties were further investigated. First, the band structure of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) was calculated by DFT. As shown in Fig. 2d, the calculated band structure shows a significant anisotropy in the divergence of the band with an indirect band gap of \(1.06\;\mathrm{eV}\) , confirming the semiconducting characteristic of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) . To clarify the origin of the electronic band structure, the partial density of states (PDOS) was further calculated to gain detailed information on the elements. As presented in Fig. 2e, the valence band maximum (VBM) mainly consisted of the p orbitals of O atoms and d orbitals of Cu atoms, while the conduction band minimum (CBM) comprised the dominating contributions from p orbitals of C and O atoms. The corresponding band-decomposed partial charge densities of the VBM and CBM are depicted in Fig. 2f. In
addition, the electrical conductivity of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) bulk was also measured on a pressed pellet using a two-contact probe method. As shown in Fig. 2c, current-voltage curves reveal a conductivity of \(1.76\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{S\,m^{-1}}\) for \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) at room temperature, much higher than that of HHTP and THQ monomers (Fig. S13), demonstrating its excellent electrical conductivity.
Inspired by its excellent structural features, high electrical conductivity, and eximious chemical stability, the potential application of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) for \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) storage was investigated. The electrochemical behavior of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) as an anode material for LIBs was evaluated in a potential window of \(0.01{-}3.0\ \mathrm{V}\) (vs. \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/L i)}\) in R2032-type coin cells, where lithium metal and \(1.0\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) \(\mathrm{LiF}_{6}\) in EC/DEC/DMC (1:1:1 volume ratio) acted as the counter electrode and electrolyte, respectively. First, CV measurement was performed to gain an elementary understanding of the redox behavior of the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) electrode. As shown in Fig. 3a, two reduction peaks at the potentials of 1.10 and \(0.64\:\mathrm{V}\) (vs. \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/L i)}\) are found for the first cathodic sweep, corresponding to a multistep \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) intercalation process for the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) anode. The irreversible peak at around \(1.10\;\mathrm{V}\) can be attributed to the reduction reaction of surface-active materials, which is caused by the formation of the solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) film [26]. The peak at a potential with \(0.64\:\mathrm{V}\) corresponds to the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) intercalation on \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{O}\) sites [27]. In addition, the inclined area below \(0.5\;\mathrm{V}\) is ascribed to the loading of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) on the benzene rings of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) [28,29]. Subsequently, during the anodic scan, the peaks located at \({\sim}0.51\) and \({\sim}1.71\mathrm{~V~}\) are observed,
corresponding to the delithiation procedure of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})\) - (THQ) [30]. Notably, the CV curves almost overlap in the following cycles, indicating the high reversibility of redox reactions in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) .
To further evaluate the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) storage capacity of the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) electrode, galvanostatic charge-discharge (GCD) measurements were performed under a current density of \(50\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) . As presented in Fig. 3b, \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) exhibits initial discharge/charge capacities of \(1218.9/\) \(705.4\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , corresponding to a Coulombic efficiency of \(57.9\%\) . The low Coulombic efficiency in the first cycle is attributed to the electrolyte decomposition and formation of SEI film on the porous \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) -based electrode surface [31,32], which can be improved by the method of optimizing the electrolyte composition, using SEI-forming additives, prelithiating the electrode, etc. [33–36]. The discharge/charge capacities at the 2nd, 3rd, 4th, and 5th cycles are 734.7/686.5, 734.1/695.2, 725.1/695.7, and \(726.0/699.6\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) with Coulombic efficiencies of \(93.4\%\) , \(94.7\%\) , \(95.9\%\) , and \(96.4\%\) , respectively. In addition, the long-time cycle performance of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) was carried out at \(300\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) . As presented in Fig. 3e, the capacity of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) decreased in the first few cycles and then increased, which is possibly attributed to the sufficient permeation of the electrolyte and activated process of the electrode material [10]. During the cycling, \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})\) -(THQ) could deliver a reversible capacity of \(624.8\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(300\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and capacity retention of \(87.1\%\) after 200 cycles, accompanied by a high Coulombic efficiency of about \(100\%\) and a low-capacity deterioration rate of \(0.06\%\) per
cycle, which are superior to some reported single-ligand 2D c-MOFs-based electrode materials [26,37] and dual-ligand materials [30,38]. In addition, the rate capability of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})\) - (THQ) was also investigated. As presented in Fig. 3d, at varied current densities of 50, 150, 300, 600, 900, 1200, and \(1500\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})\) - (THQ) provides reversible capacities of 725.1, 550.5, 508.2, 443.7, 409.5, 379.9, and \(347.0\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively, indicating excellent rate performance. In addition, after returning the current density from 1500 to \(50\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , the capacity of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) could quickly recover, presenting excellent tolerance to the rapid lithiation/delithiation process.
For comparison, the electrochemical behaviors of HHTP and THQ monomers were also evaluated under the same conditions (Figs S14 and S15). Compared with \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) , HHTP and THQ monomers represented lower initial capacities of 456.0 and \(993.1\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(50\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively, and continued to decay in the following charge/discharge process. Cyclic performance tests at \(300\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) current density show that the HHTP and THQ monomers can only provide limited reversible capacities of 185.0 and \(381.8\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively. In addition, after 200 cycles, only \(26.9\%\) and \(48.5\%\) capacities can be retained and the corresponding capacity deterioration rates are as high as \(0.36\%\) and \(0.26\%\) per cycle for HHTP and THQ, respectively, indicating their poor cycling stability. Moreover, the rate capabilities of HHTP and THQ monomers exhibit huge attenuation upon increasing the current density. As shown in Fig. 3c, the low reversible capacities of 294.0/494.3, 170.5/364.8, 111.2/309.4, 72.5/252.9, 56.2/227.2, 48.6/209.9, and \(42.9/194.8\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for HHTP and THQ were observed at current densities of 50, 150, 300, 600, 900, 1200, and \(1500\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively, which are significantly inferior to \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) .
To clarify the superior electrochemical performance of
\(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) , EIS, self-discharge, and solubility measurements were performed on \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) , HHTP, and THQ electrodes. As shown in Fig. 4a, the Nyquist plots of the three electrodes are comprised of the semicircle in the medium-high-frequency field and the upward-sloping line in the low-frequency field, corresponding to the charge transfer resistance \(\left(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)\) and Warburg diffusion resistance \((Z_{\mathrm{W}})\) , respectively [39,40]. It is noteworthy that the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) possesses a smaller \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) than those of the HHTP and THQ monomers, indicating the good conductivity of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) facilitates the rapid interface charge transfer. The self-discharge test manifests that the open-circuit potential of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) has almost no attenuation within 20 days (Fig. 4b), indicating no evident self-discharge occurred for \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) . Furthermore, after 20 days of storage, nearly \(100\%\) of the initial capacity was still retained, demonstrating its remarkable stability in the fully charged state. In contrast to \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) , both HHTP and THQ exhibit an obvious self-discharge phenomenon during the storage process, especially for HHTP, where the voltage continuously drops from 2.96 to \(0.16\;\mathrm{V}\) in a short time (Fig. S16). The solubility test shows that the HHTP and THQ electrodes are dissolved after soaking in the electrolyte for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) , while the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) electrode exhibits satisfactory insolubility in the corresponding electrolyte (Fig. S17), indicating that the coordination of HHTP and THQ with \(C\mathbf{u}^{2+}\) to form \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) could effectively inhibit their dissolution in the electrolyte. Based on the above results, it can be concluded that improved stability and electrical conductivity may be responsible for the superior electrochemical performance of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) .
To further understand the charge-storage mechanism of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) , CV measurements at scan rates from 0.2 to
\(1.0\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) were carried out. As shown in Fig. 4c, the current intensities of CV curves increase with the rise of scan rates. The relationship between the measured current \((i)\) and the scan rates \((\nu)\) in CV can be analyzed by the equation of \(i=a\nu^{b}\), where \(a\) and \(b\) are adjustable constants that can be deduced by fitting \(\log(i)\) versus \(\log(\nu)\) [26,41]. Generally, the result of the \(b\) -value tends to 0.5, suggesting a diffusion-controlled kinetic process, while the \(b\) -value tends to 1.0, indicating a capacitive-dominant process. As shown in Fig. 4d, the \(b\) -values of the cathodic and anodic peaks are calculated to be 0.83 and 0.86, respectively, indicating that the coexistence of diffusion and capacitive controlled charge storage processes in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\). The mechanism from these two processes can be quantitatively described by the formula of \(i=k_{1}\nu+k_{2}\nu^{1/2}\), where \(k_{1}\) and \(k_{2}\) are constants determined by fitting \(\nu^{1/2}\) versus \(i/\nu^{1/2}\) [42,43]. Therefore, the diffusion-controlled contribution \((k_{2}\nu^{1/2})\) and capacitive-controlled contribution \((k_{1}\nu)\) to the whole capacity were further calculated. As shown in Fig. 4e, the dark shaded area represents the capacitive effect, and the corresponding capacitive contribution ratios increase from \(59.5\%\) to \(78.4\%\) as the scan rate increases from 0.2 to \(1.0\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) (Fig. 4f). These results consequently suggest that the capacity of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) is contributed by both pseudocapacitive process and intercalation reaction, thus achieving a high specific capacity and fast kinetics.
To explore the lithium storage mechanism of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})\)-(THQ), ex-situ FT-IR and XPS measurements were conducted at different discharge/charge states to monitor the composition variation in the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) electrode (Fig. 5a). As illustrated in Fig. 5b, the FT-IR spectrum of the pristine \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) anode shows characteristic peaks at 1654 and \(1585\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), attributed to the \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) and the aromatic \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{C}\) groups, respectively [44,45]. These peaks gradually weaken with continuous discharge to \(0.01\;\mathrm{V}\), corresponding to the insertion of \(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\)at the \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) and aromatic \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{C}\) sites, forming the \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{Li}\) and \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{Li}\) species [45]. Significantly, after again charging to \(3.0\;\mathrm{V}\), the intensity of these characteristic peaks can recover to the initial state, demonstrating high reversibility of the interaction between the active sites and \(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\). Moreover, a similar reversible variation of the \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) group can also be observed in the XPS spectra of O 1s. As shown in Fig. 5c, the HR-XPS O 1s spectrum for the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) electrode at the initial state exhibits two peaks at 533.0 and \(531.4\;\mathrm{eV}\), which are assigned to the \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{O}\) bonds, respectively [46–48]. When the electrode is gradually discharged to \(0.01\;\mathrm{V}\), the \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) peak intensity significantly reduces while the \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{O}\) peak intensity gradually increases. In the following charging process, the intensity of \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) gradually recovers. These results further confirm that the \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) group in the \(\mathrm{CuO}_{4}\) unit is the redox-active site for lithium storage. In addition, the valence variation of the central metal in the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) structure during the discharge/charge process was also investigated. As displayed in HR-XPS Cu 2p spectra, the \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) signals at 934.6 and \(954.6\;\mathrm{eV}\) in a pristine state entirely vanish after full discharge to \(0.01\;\mathrm{V}\) (Fig. 5d), suggesting the reduction of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\). In the subsequent charging process, the opposite evolution did not occur due to the oxidation of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)requires a voltage higher than \(3.0\;\mathrm{V}\) [17].
