 of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\) requires a voltage higher than \(3.0\;\mathrm{V}\) [17].
To deeply understand the lithium storage mechanism of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\), DFT calculations were carried out. First, the surface electrostatic potential of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) was afforded by theoretical calculations. As shown in Fig. 5f, the O sites in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) are the strongest electronegative, followed by the benzene rings of HHTP, indicating that both \(\mathrm{CuO}_{4}\) and
benzene rings can function as the active sites for \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) storage, which is consistent with the experimental results. In principle, the per asymmetric unit of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) can accommodate \(6\,\mathrm{\,Li^{+}}\) at the \({\mathrm{CuO}}_{4}\) sites [11] (denoted as \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})\) - \((\mathrm{THQ})~+~6\mathrm{Li})\) and \(3\,\mathrm{\:Li^{+}}\) at the benzene rings sites [10,49] (denoted as \(\mathrm{Cu_{3}(H H T P)(T H Q)\ +\ 3L i)}\) . In total, \(9\,\mathrm{\:Li^{+}}\) can be received by \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) (denoted as \(\mathrm{Cu_{3}(H H T P)(T H Q)}\) \(+\ 9\mathrm{Li})\) based on its dual active sites. To determine the lithiation sequence on these active sites, three lithiated models of \(\mathrm{Cu_{3}(H H T P)(T H Q)}~~+~~3\mathrm{Li},\) , \(\mathrm{Cu_{3}(H H T P)(T H Q)}~~+~~6\mathrm{Li},\) , and \(\mathrm{Cu_{3}(H H T P)(T H Q)}~+~9\mathrm{Li}\) were established (Fig. S18), and the
corresponding \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) absorption energies on these models were calculated (Table S1). As can be found, the absorption energy value for \(\mathrm{Cu_{3}(H H T P)(T H Q)+6L i}\) is significantly more negative than \(\mathrm{Cu_{3}(H H T P)(T H Q)+3L i},\) indicating that \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) preferentially bind to the \({\mathrm{C}}{=}\mathrm{O}\) active sites in the \({\mathrm{CuO}}_{4}\) unit. Accordingly, a two-step lithiation procedure for one cell of \(\mathrm{Cu_{3}(H H T P)(T H Q)}\) was proposed: \(\mathrm{Cu_{3}(H H T P)(T H Q)\rightarrow C u_{3}(H H T P)(T H Q)\ +\ 6L i}\) \(\rightarrow\mathrm{\Cu_{3}(H H T P)(T H Q)\ +\ 9L i}\) (Fig. 5g). Moreover, the total energies along the above route were calculated, and it is found that the total energies drop sharply in the first stage and gently in the second stage, which is consistent with the trend of the
discharge curve (Fig. 5e).
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, a facile one-step synthetic method has been developed for preparing dual-ligand-based 2D c-MOF \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) with excellent electrical conductivity, abundant active sites, and high stability. These merits endow the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) anode with eminent lithium storage performance in LIBs in terms of high specific capacity, excellent rate performance, and long-term cycling stability. In addition, combining spectroscopic analyses and DFT calculations reveals that both \(\mathrm{CuO}_{4}\) units and aromatic benzene rings in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})(\mathrm{THQ})\) are involved in the lithium storage reaction. This work not only gives insight into the construction of dual ligand-based 2D c-MOFs, but also provides a new avenue for the development of high-performance electrode materials.
